Sign-up policy rehearsal: 13 sign-ups (5 good: paid or activated; 2 junk: flagged or hard-bounced; 6 with no outcome)

RULE                             WOULD BLOCK  GOOD LOST  JUNK STOPPED  NO OUTCOME  WHAT IT DOES
reject_syntax                    1            0          0             1           Reject addresses with invalid syntax
require_mx                       3            0          1             2           Reject domains that cannot receive mail
reject_invalid                   4            0          1             3           Reject what basic mode calls invalid (the two rules above)
block_disposable                 2            1          1             0           Block disposable domains
block_legacy                     1            1          0             0           Block domains of discontinued providers
block_free                       0            0          0             0           Block free webmail (Gmail, Outlook, Yahoo...)
reject_invalid_block_disposable  6            1          2             3           Reject invalid and block disposable (the documented sign-up pattern)
mailbox_reject_invalid           5            0          1             4           Reject what mailbox mode calls invalid
mailbox_reject_invalid_risky     10           3          2             5           Reject invalid and risky in mailbox mode

Mailbox rules use the 6 of 13 addresses that got an answer from the API.
Read with care: addresses are checked as they are today, not as they were on the sign-up date; sign-ups that an earlier rule already rejected are not in your data; and small counts prove little.
